MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I just wrote a first-order Koyck model in Stan along with
a simulator in R.  It all seems to work without a hitch.
Here are the links to the code:

   R Simulator:
   https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/misc/garch/koyck-sim.R

   Stan Model:
   https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/misc/garch/koyck.stan

The model (which is very much like Mark Leeds's) is short enough to dump
out in the mail:

   // geometric lag time-series (Koyck 1951)
   //

   data {
     int<lower=0> T;   // number of time points
     real y[T];        // output at time t
     real x[T];        // predictor for time t
   }
   parameters {
     real alpha;                       // intercept
     real beta;                        // slope
     real <lower=0, upper=1> lambda;   // lag
     real <lower=0> sigma;             // noise scale
   }
   model {
     alpha ~ cauchy(0,5);
     beta ~ cauchy(0,5);
     lambda ~ uniform(0,1);
     sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);
     for (t in 2:T)
       y[t] ~ normal(alpha +  beta * x[t] +  lambda * y[t-1],
                     sigma);
   }

I followed the parameterization in:

   http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributed_lag

I used proper priors for everything, but fairly diffuse ones.
You could eliminate the uniform one, but having it isn't going
to slow things down noticeably.

One difference from Mike Leeds's Stan model is that I used an
intercept term alpha.   Without an intercept, the outputs should
be centered (this could be done in Stan's transformed data block).

Here's the simulator in R:

   library("rstan");

   T <- 200;
   x <- rnorm(T,0,1);
   alpha <- -1.25;
   beta <- 0.75;
   lambda <- 0.6;
   sigma <- 0.5;

   y <- rep(NA,T);
   y[1] <- rnorm(1,alpha + beta * x[1],sigma);
   for (t in 2:T)
     y[t] <- rnorm(1,alpha + beta * x[t] + lambda * y[t-1], sigma);

   fit <- stan(file="koyck.stan", data=c("T","y","x"), iter=500, chains=4, fit=fit);

And here's the output, which is pretty much instant after the mode
compiles:

   Inference for Stan model: koyck.
   4 chains: each with iter=500; warmup=250; thin=1; 500 iterations saved.

           mean se_mean   sd  2.5%   25%   50%   75% 97.5% n_eff Rhat
   alpha  -1.18    0.01 0.11 -1.38 -1.24 -1.18 -1.10 -0.97   287 1.01
   beta    0.73    0.00 0.03  0.66  0.70  0.73  0.75  0.79   312 1.01
   lambda  0.63    0.00 0.03  0.57  0.61  0.63  0.65  0.69   275 1.01
   sigma   0.49    0.00 0.02  0.45  0.47  0.49  0.51  0.54   277 1.01
   lp__   39.73    0.09 1.36 36.17 39.01 39.99 40.78 41.51   256 1.00

I also tried a lambda=0.98 setting with 10K data points and
everything else the same, and here's the fit:

              mean se_mean    sd     2.5%      25%      50%      75%    97.5% n_eff  Rhat
   alpha    -1.269   0.003 0.058   -1.386   -1.312   -1.270   -1.229   -1.156   429 1.004
   beta      0.750   0.000 0.005    0.741    0.747    0.750    0.753    0.759  1183 1.002
   lambda    0.980   0.000 0.001    0.978    0.979    0.980    0.980    0.981   428 1.005
   sigma     0.504   0.000 0.003    0.497    0.501    0.504    0.506    0.511  1065 1.003
   lp__   1857.899   0.063 1.353 1854.309 1857.265 1858.221 1858.858 1859.628   465 1.011

So I think it's safe to say that Stan can fit the Koyck model :-)

It's slower with a very high lambda, but NUTS's adaptation still
figures it out.   The model could be made quite a bit more efficient
with vectorization.

- Bob

