> >     Overall, the model won't make sense in a Bayesian setting with the
> >     parameterization (1-lambda)* beta * x[n].  The problem is that
> >     beta needs to get huge when lambda gets close to 1, so what kind
> >     of prior makes sense for it?
> >
> >     Putting a prior on beta makes much more sense.
> >
> >
> > I think it is actually the opposite: when the autoregressive parameter (lambda) gets close to 1, beta needs to go to
> > zero.
>
> ??  The Wikipedia had a simple coefficient in the regression:
>
>    beta_raw * x[n]
>
> If we change the model to include the term
>
>     beta * (1 - lambda) * x[n]
>
> then
>
>     beta = beta_raw / (1 - lambda).
>
> And so if beta_raw > 0, then as lambda -> 1, we have
> beta -> +infinity.
>
> Or am I totally missing something here?  (Wouldn't be
> the first time!)

If beta_raw were independent of lambda, then yes. But I don't think we should consider these priors to be independent. If you write it like

beta = beta_raw(lambda) / (1 - lambda)

where beta_raw is a function of lambda, and want beta to approach 0 as lambda approaches 1, then making the prior scale be (2.5 * (1 - lambda^2))^2  is one way to do that.

lambda <-seq(from = .Machine$double.eps, to = 1 - .Machine$double.eps, length.out = 1000)
foo <- function(l) sd(rnorm(10000, 0, (2.5 * (1 - l^2))^2) / (1 - l))
plot(lambda, sapply(lambda, foo), type = "l", ylab = "SD of beta") # attached

Ben

