On Thursday, April 11, 2013 7:24:10 PM UTC-4, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Thursday, April 11, 2013 2:59:13 PM UTC-4, Mark Leeds wrote:
> > #=========================================================================================
> > # STAN CODE
> > #===============================================================================================================================================
> >
> >
> > data {
> >   int<lower=0> T; // number of time points
> >   real y[T]; // output at time t
> >   real x[T]; // predictor for time t
> > }
> > parameters {
> >   real beta1mlambda; // equals beta * (1-lambda)
> >   real <lower=0, upper=1> lambda; // lag
> >   real <lower=0> sigma; // noise scale
> > }
> >
> > model {
> >   beta1mlambda ~ cauchy(0,5);
> >   lambda ~ uniform(0,1);
> >   sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);
> >   y[1] ~  normal(beta1mlambda * x[1], sigma);
> >   for (t in 2:T)
> >     y[t] ~ normal( beta1mlambda * x[t] + lambda * y[t-1],sigma);
> > }
> >
> > generated quantities {
> >   real beta;
> >   beta <-  beta1mlambda / (1 - lambda);
> > }
>
> I think in the PDF you sent earlier, the authors are presuming an improper prior on beta. In that case, as lambda -> 1, the marginal posterior distribution of beta is not well-defined. However, with a proper prior on beta (or beta1mlambda), then the marginal posterior distribution is just the prior distribution in the case where lambda -> 1. That would be true for Bayesian inference in general; it doesn't have anything in particular to do with HMC or Gibbs sampling or whatever algorithm you use to draw from the posterior.
>
> I think most people would want the posterior distribution of beta to converge to 0 as lambda -> 1, which implies that the prior for beta1mlambda can't be independent of lambda. I would do this:
>
> data {
>   int<lower=0> T; // number of time points
>   real y[T]; // output at time t
>   real x[T]; // predictor for time t
> }
> parameters {
>   real beta1mlambda; // equals beta * (1-lambda)
>   real <lower=0, upper=1> lambda; // lag
>   real <lower=0> sigma; // noise scale
> }
>
> model {
>   real mu[T];
>   beta1mlambda ~ normal(0,square(2.236 * (1 - square(lambda))));
>   // implicit: lambda ~ uniform(0,1);
>   sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);
>   mu[1] <- y[1]
>   for (t in 2:T)
>     mu[t] <- beta1mlambda * x[t] + lambda * y[t-1];
>
>   y ~ normal(mu, sigma);
> }
> generated quantities {
>   real beta;
>   beta <-  beta1mlambda / (1 - lambda);
> }
>
> Ben
>

